All 10 tests pass, including the new regression test.

## Summary

**Issue:** When an option has a string default value and an alias, the string type was not propagated to the alias. Parsing `-a 01` where `a` aliases `arg` with a string default would coerce `"01"` to the number `1`.

**Root cause:** In `lib/index.js`, the `defaults` block only added the canonical key to `opts.string` or `opts.boolean`, but not its aliases:

```js
if (defaults) {
    for (k in opts.default) {
        opts.alias[k] = opts.alias[k] || [];
        (opts[typeof opts.default[k]] || []).push(k);  // Only pushed `k`, not aliases
    }
}
```

So when the alias was used on the command line (e.g., `-a 01`), the value bypassed the string-coercion check in `toVal` and got coerced to a number.

**Fix:** Push both the canonical key and its aliases to the appropriate type-list (`opts.string` or `opts.boolean`) so the type flows through to all aliases:

```js
if (defaults) {
    for (k in opts.default) {
        opts.alias[k] = opts.alias[k] || [];
        arr = opts.alias[k].concat(k);
        for (i=0; i < arr.length; i++) {
            (opts[typeof opts.default[k]] || []).push(arr[i]);
        }
    }
}
```

This reuses the existing `arr` and `i` variables (already declared at the top of the function scope), keeping the fix minimal and idiomatic to the codebase.

